    "title": "2028 Los Angeles Olympics: How to Buy Tickets",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTOlympic Fans Get First Chance to Buy Tickets to 2028 Games in Los AngelesOrganizers began releasing blocks of tickets to buyers in April. Prices start at $28, but early buyers complained that those tickets were scarce.2028 will be the third time Los Angeles hosts the Summer Games, after 1932 and 1984.Credit...Alex Welsh for The New York TimesJesus Jiménez and Jill CowanJesus Jiménez and Jill Cowan reported from Los Angeles and have been covering preparations for the 2028 Olympics.Published Jan. 13, 2026Updated April 9, 2026Now that the 2026 Winter Olympics and Paralympics have ended, eyes are turning to Los Angeles, which will host the 2028 Summer Games.Although the Olympics are still more than two years away, ticket sales have already begun. Organizers said in early April that the 2028 Games had “significantly exceeded” the first week of sales of previous Olympics and that, for the first time, all of those tickets had gone to locals.The complex lottery process for buying tickets is meant to prioritize residents of the host cities, but also allow everyone else a fair shot at buying Olympic tickets as bots and inflated prices on resale markets have made entry to many other big events difficult to attain.Organizers say that tickets to all sports will be available at a range of prices, starting at $28.The 2028 Olympics will be Los Angeles’s third time hosting the event, after the Summer Games in 1932 and 1984. The event will also be the first Summer Games hosted in the United States since the 1996 Olympics in Atlanta.Hundreds of events will stretch across Southern California and Oklahoma City, creating many opportunities for spectators to catch a glimpse of the action. Los Angeles will also host the Paralympic Games, which will begin in August 2028.Here’s what to know about attending the 2028 Olympics.If you registered for the lottery, you’re about to get the chance to buy tickets.To get access to the first round of sales, those interested in purchasing tickets for Olympic events had to register online and create a profile. Registration, which was free, began in January.Subscribe to The Times to read as many articles as you like.Jesus Jiménez is a Times reporter covering North Texas. He is based in Dallas.Jill Cowan is a Times reporter based in Los Angeles, covering the forces shaping life in Southern California and throughout the state.A version of this article appears in print on Jan. 1, 2026, Section B, Page 12 of the New York edition with the headline: How to Register for Tickets to 2028 Games in Los Angeles (and Oklahoma). Order Reprints | Today’s Paper | SubscribeRelated ContentAdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
